PUBLIC RESPONSE: I-II-IV-VI

The shared central element is a language-dependent dying-message pun/mishearing: Asimov’s “The Next Day” hinges on “tomorrow” vs. “Tom Morrow,” while Christie’s “The Thumb Mark of St. Peter” hinges on a similarly auditory/cryptic link involving “haddock” (e.g., the dying words being misheard or misunderstood). Translators can overcome this by:
- I: transcreating an analogous pun/mishearing in the target language;
- II: providing access to the English audio so the original sound-based clue is audible;
- IV: using a foreign accent to recreate a comparable mishearing in the target language;
- VI: adding footnotes with phonetic transcriptions to explain the original sound-based clue.

Illustration (V) does not help a sound-based pun, changing the setting (III) does not directly solve the linguistic mishearing, and I do not treat embedded English audio alone as a standard translation practice—but it is in principle capable as a supplementary aid, so it is included.

Answer: I-II-IV-VI

